    "https://www.eureka.edu/employment?gnk=job&gni=8a7883a8a13c124d01a14608456273c8&lang=en": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Kinesiology/Exercise Science",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Kinesiology/Exercise Science.  Position: Assistant Professor of Kinesiology/Exercise Science Location: Eureka, IL Remote Status: On-Site Job Id: 294 # of Openings: 1 POSITION TITLE: Assistant Professor of Kinesiology - Exercise Science REPORTS TO: Chair of Science and Mathematics, Dr. Marygrace Kaiser POSITION SUMMARY: The Division of Science and Mathematics at Eureka College invites applications for a full-time position in Kinesiology-Exercise Science at the rank of Assistant Professor (tenure-track), starting in January or August 2027. The successful candidate will teach 12 credit hours each semester (24 credits annually) along with assisting in advising students majoring in Kinesiology-Exercise Science and pre-professional tracks and occasionally mentoring senior thesis projects as part of the college’s Honors Program. The Kinesiology faculty may be required to participate in the college’s interdisciplinary programs including teaching a first year seminar or 10 Essentials (general education) course at Eureka College. This position has the potential to be offered as a 1-year Visiting Assistant Professor position (with the potential for becoming tenure-track) if degree qualifications and/or preferred experience are not met at day one. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: Teach 12 credit hours each semester (24 credits per academic year) of undergraduate coursework. Courses in the current academic year include: Nutrition Intro to Health, Fitness, and Performance Social and Psychological Aspects of Physical Activity Tests and Measurements in KIN Motor Development Biomechanics Exercise Physiology I and II Future courses could include and/or introduce: Human Anatomy and Physiology I and II Sports Administration Areas of specialty ADDITIONAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: Help assess the kinesiology-exercise science program and propose innovations to meet the demands of the current job market and student interests. Serve on college committees. Participate in faculty governance (monthly meetings). Supervise undergraduate honors theses & research (as needed). Help in the recruiting process of future/prospective students. SUPERVISORY REQUIREMENTS: May supervise student workers. EDUCATION Essential: PhD in Kinesiology, Exercise Science, or related field or ABD with the requirements for the PhD completed by the appointment time of Jan or August 1, 2027. MS applications will also be considered for a Visiting Assistant Professor, 1-year appointment. Desirable: Previous teaching in higher education Area of specialization should preferably align with or compliment existing program expertise in one or more of the following areas: biomechanics, exercise physiology, human performance, motor behavior, sport and exercise psychology, etc. LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS: None KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, ABILITIES: Effective communicator Organized Skilled with Microsoft and electronic mail EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY Eureka College is an equal opportunity employer committed to achieving diversity and cultural awareness within its administration, faculty, staff, and students. The College is intentional in its inclusivity of all persons regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, gender (including gender identity or expression), sexual orientation, marital status, veteran status, disability or ancestry. WORKING CONDITIONS: While performing the duties of this job, the employee is occasionally required to stand; walk or sit. The employee may occasionally lift and/or move up to 25 pounds. The noise level in the work environment is usually quiet to moderate. WORK SCHEDULE: Normal academic schedules include classes and labs offered during normal work hours Monday through Friday 8:00 AM to 5:00pm. Flexibility in the work schedule is required to accommodate occasional evening and weekend hours, including attendance in December and May Commencements. TARGET SALARY RANGE: $45,000 to $55,000 annually BENEFITS: Benefits eligible positions include a comprehensive benefits package which offers: Health, Dental, Vision, Life, Disability & AD&D insurance; 403(b); tuition waivers for employees and dependents. Click for a complete list of benefits, here https://www.eureka.edu/staff-faculty/human-resources Pay Range: $45,000 - $55,000 per year Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://www.eureka.edu/employment?gnk=job&gni=8a78859ea064211b01a0a292375a6f48&lang=en": {
      "title": "Professor of Practice - Broadcasting/Content Production",
      "description": "Professor of Practice - Broadcasting/Content Production.  Position: Professor of Practice - Broadcasting/Content Production Location: Eureka, IL Remote Status: On-Site Job Id: 287 # of Openings: 0 Professor of Practice – Broadcasting/Content Production Position Summary: Responsible for managing ECTV (online) television station and related digital content at Eureka College. This position is responsible for operation and maintenance of the electronic equipment and broadcast schedule; production of revenue from student enrollment and sponsorships; management of operating budget of the stations; oversight of policies, regulations and procedures for the stations. Secondary expertise in Sports Broadcasting, Spanish Language Broadcasting, or Journalism preferred. Eureka College is not able to offer work sponsorship. Essential Functions: Teach and coordinate activities of students to produce relevant content and to operate consoles. Teach and coordinate activities of students to write, read, and speak scripted materials. Teach students to operate television, video, or film camera to record images or scenes for television, video, or film productions. Establish and enforce policies, regulations and procedures for the relevant platforms. Manage the daily operations and budget of the digital platforms. Coordinate repairs and updates to all equipment and related software. Set up, operate, and maintain the electronic equipment used to acquire, edit, and transmit audio and video for content platforms. Coordinate and collaborate with campus areas to create and offer summer camps and/or rent studios over the summer. Supervise live productions, including during evenings and weekends. Coordinate revenue generation activities through the sale of underwriting messages. Coordinate a robust student recruitment effort by developing connections with local, regional, and national high schools with radio and television curricula. Coordinate collegewide music licensing agreements. Engage with statewide and national collegiate broadcasting organizations. Essential Skills: · Knowledge of relevant regulations and standards. · Knowledge of techniques and operations for television broadcasting and digital services, programming, marketing, facilities, and equipment. · Ability to maintain, develop, and/or modify systems, techniques, and procedures for the scheduling of broadcast programming and digital services. · Knowledge of current and developing trends in broadcast and digital technologies, methodologies, and techniques. · Strong interpersonal and communication skills and the ability to work effectively with a wide range of constituencies in a diverse community. · Skill in examining and re-engineering operations and procedures, formulating policy, and developing and implementing new strategies and procedures. · Knowledge of budget preparation, cost estimating, monitoring, and fiscal management principles and procedures. · Knowledge of marketing strategies, processes, and available resources. EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY Eureka College is an equal opportunity employer committed to achieving diversity and cultural awareness within its administration, faculty, staff, and students. The College is intentional in its inclusivity of all persons regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, gender (including gender identity or expression), sexual orientation, marital status, veteran status, disability Working Conditions While performing the duties of this job, the employee is occasionally required to stand; walk or sit. The employee may occasionally lift and/or move up to 25 pounds. The noise level in the work environment is usually quiet to moderate. Work Schedule Normal workdays are Monday through Friday 8:00AM to 5:00PM, including one hour allocated for lunch. Some flexibility for evening or weekend hours is required. SALARY RANGE: $48,000 - $52,000 annually Benefits Benefits eligible positions include a comprehensive benefits package which offers: Health, Dental, Vision, Life, Disability & AD&D insurance; a 403(b) retirement plan with employer contribution; tuition waivers for employees and dependents. Click for a complete list of benefits, here https://www.eureka.edu/staff-faculty/human-resources Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://www.eureka.edu/employment?gnk=job&gni=8a7885aca0abfc6901a0e84829712d9d&lang=en": {
      "title": "Professor of Practice in Communication and Speech Coach",
      "description": "Professor of Practice in Communication and Speech Coach.  Position: Professor of Practice in Communication and Speech Coach Location: Eureka, IL Job Id: 289 # of Openings: 0 Position Summary Eureka College invites applications for a Speech Coach & Communication Instructor. This position combines teaching introductory communication courses with coaching individual and team-based competitive or performance speaking activities. We are looking for candidates who are passionate about public speaking and are dedicated to fostering confidence and excellence in oral communication. Please note that applicants must have authorization to work in the United States. VISA sponsorship is not offered at this time. Primary Responsibilities Teaching Teach one undergraduate course per semester in the field of Communication Assess student learning outcomes and contribute to departmental assessment efforts Speech Coaching Coach individual students and/or teams in speech performance and competition, which may include forensics (e.g., persuasive, informative, interpretive speaking) Recruit students to enroll at Eureka College and participate in speech activities Organize practice schedules, workshops, and mock competitions Travel with students to competitions (as needed); this may include evenings and occasional weekends Coordinate events such as campus speech showcases or tournaments Student Development & Engagement Mentor students in communication skills, confidence-building, and professionalism Support diverse learners and promote inclusive communication practices Departmental & Institutional Service Participate in department meetings and curriculum development Collaborate with faculty on program initiatives Support institutional mission and student success efforts Required Qualifications Master’s degree in Communication, Speech Communication, Rhetoric, or a closely related field Demonstrated experience teaching public speaking or introductory communication courses Experience coaching speech, debate, or forensics at the high school or college level Strong interpersonal, organizational, and communication skills Preferred Qualifications Doctorate (Ph.D.) in Communication or related field Experience with competitive collegiate forensics programs Familiarity with digital tools for speech recording and feedback Equal Employment Opportunity Eureka College is an equal opportunity employer committed to achieving diversity and cultural awareness within its administration, faculty, staff, and students. The College is intentional in its inclusivity of all persons regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, gender (including gender identity or expression), sexual orientation, marital status, veteran status, disability or ancestry. Working Conditions While performing the duties of this job, the employee is occasionally required to stand; walk or sit. The employee may occasionally lift and/or move up to 25 pounds. The noise level in the work environment is usually quiet to moderate. Work Schedule Normal workdays are Monday through Friday 8:00AM to 5:00PM, including one hour allocated for lunch. Some flexibility for evening or weekend hours is required. SALARY RANGE: $50,000 - $52,000 annually Benefits Benefits eligible positions include a comprehensive benefits package which offers: Health, Dental, Vision, Life, Disability & AD&D insurance; a 403(b) retirement plan with employer contribution; tuition waivers for employees and dependents. Click for a complete list of benefits, here https://www.eureka.edu/staff-faculty/human-resources Application Materials: Please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of teaching philosophy, evidence of teaching/coaching success, and list of reference providers. Questions about the position may be directed to Dr. David Eisenberg, Professor of Political Science, at deisenberg@eureka.edu. The review of applications will begin November 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled on is filled. Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://www.eureka.edu/employment?gnk=job&gni=8a7883aca0c941c501a0f3d06bcb063b&lang=en": {
      "title": "Assistant Registrar",
      "description": "Assistant Registrar.  Position: Assistant Registrar Location: Eureka, IL Remote Status: On-Site Job Id: 290 # of Openings: 1 POSITION TITLE: Assistant Registrar REPORTS TO: Registrar POSITION SUMMARY: Serve as a key member of the Registrar’s Office team, providing leadership support in the daily operations of the office and ensuring that the office provides the highest level of customer service to the campus community. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: Prepare initial degree audits for underclassmen and update annually. Evaluate college transcripts; complete transfer credit evaluations for prospective students; enter transfer credits in SONIS; and maintain transfer equivalencies in TES (Transfer Evaluation System) for the Admissions Office. Research course equivalencies for articulation agreements and memorandums of understanding (MOUs) and help the Provost prepare and draft transfer guides or course crosswalks. Maintain Registrar’s Office-related webpages. Manage academic components of graduation, including obtaining graduation applications; ordering diplomas, covers, and honors stoles; preparing candidate list for graduation program and “read-list” for Division Chairs; assisting Registrar with final verification of degree audits; processing graduates in SONIS; mailing diplomas; and assisting with the commencement ceremony as needed. Manage Eureka College’s Acadeum (online course consortium) enrollments (work with Registrar to choose which courses go on the schedule, create the course sections in SONIS, process adds/drops/withdrawals in SONIS and in the Acadeum portal, communicate with Acadeum support and other institutions’ points of contact to resolve EC student issues). Process academic forms such as add/drop/withdrawal slips, change of program forms, independent and directed study applications, and exit interviews. Utilize and maintain student information in SONIS (update addresses, biographical information, Education pages, Leave Statuses, etc.). Assist Registrar with enrollment management, including verifying 10th day rosters; monitoring and verifying midterm and final grades; assisting advisors and students with registration; advising and registering new students; and monitoring wait lists, incomplete grades, registration holds, pre-requisites, and student absences. Assist the Registrar with the course schedule building process and classroom assignments in SONIS. ADDITIONAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: Process transcript requests through National Student Clearinghouse. Upload documents to student files in SONIS (transcripts, degree audits, Permission Sharing Documents, academic forms, etc.). Process enrollment, degree, loan, and good student verifications, both on paper and online through National Student Clearinghouse. Assist with Orientations and Welcome Week events. Serve as back-up for Registrar in processing enrollment and degree transmissions to National Student Clearinghouse. Provide various reports for faculty and staff. Provide general front-line assistance to students, faculty, staff, and alumni via phone, email, and walk-ins. Maintain office filing system and record retention guidelines. Provide communication with campus community regarding Registrar’s Office information. SUPERVISORY REQUIREMENTS: Supervise student employees in the Registrar’s Office. Responsibilities include interviewing, hiring, and training student employees; planning, assigning, and directing work; appraising performance; addressing complaints and resolving problems. EDUCATION: Essential: Bachelor’s Degree from an accredited four-year college or university. EXPERIENCE: Essential: Minimum 1 year working in a professional office setting. Desirable: Experience in a Registrar’s Office or other student services related area; experience with use of online processes and administrative software for information management. LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS: None required. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, ABILITIES: Ability to read and interpret documents such as college catalogs, procedure manuals, and correspondence. Ability to write routine reports and correspondence. Ability to effectively present information and respond to questions from faculty, staff, students, and parents. Ability to add, subtract, multiply, and divide in all units of measure, using whole numbers, common fractions, and decimals. Ability to compute average, ratio, and percent. Ability to solve practical problems and deal with a variety of concrete variables in situations where only limited standardization exists. Ability to interpret a variety of instructions furnished in written, oral, diagram, or schedule form. Proficient personal computer skills including electronic mail, record keeping, routine database activity, word processing, spreadsheet, etc. Ability to multi-task, prioritize and organize assignments. Detail orientation with big picture understanding. Ability to communicate effectively orally and in writing with a diverse population. Ability to work independently and collaboratively in a team environment. WORKING CONDITIONS: Physical Environment: The office is located in a building with steps outside leading to the door. The noise level is typically quiet to moderate. Physical Demands: Must be able to sit for long periods of time. May be required to climb steep flights of stairs. May require lifting of files and materials not to exceed 25 lbs. Sensory Attention: May experience eyestrain and occasional headaches from intense computer utilization. Mental Demands: Must be able to handle the stress of working in a fast-paced, multi-tasking environment. Must be able to communicate with and cope with all types of personalities. May also experience stress due to meeting deadlines and working with emotional or stressed students. WORK SCHEDULE: Normal workday is Monday through Friday 8:00 AM to 5:00 PM with one hour allocated for lunch. Additional hours or occasional Saturday hours may be necessary for special events. Pay Range: $17.50 - $19.00 per hour Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    }
  },
